<ThreatModel xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model" xmlns:i="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:ns1="http://schemas.microsoft.com/2003/10/Serialization/" xmlns:ns2="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts" xmlns:ns3="http://schemas.microsoft.com/2003/10/Serialization/Arrays" xmlns:ns5="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase" xmlns:ns6="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Interfaces"><DrawingSurfaceList><DrawingSurfaceModel ns1:Id="i1"><ns2:GenericTypeId>DRAWINGSURFACE</ns2:GenericTypeId><ns2:Guid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</ns2:Guid><ns2:Properties><ns3:anyType i:type="b:HeaderDisplayAttribute"><ns5:DisplayName>Diagram</ns5:DisplayName><ns5:Name /><ns5:Value i:nil="true" /></ns3:anyType><ns3:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute"><ns5:DisplayName>Name</ns5:DisplayName><ns5:Name /><ns5:Value i:type="c:string">Diagram 1</ns5:Value></ns3:anyType><ns3:anyType i:type="b:HeaderDisplayAttribute"><ns5:DisplayName>Custom Attributes</ns5:DisplayName><ns5:Name /><ns5:Value i:nil="true" /></ns3:anyType><ns3:anyType i:type="b:CustomStringDisplayAttribute"><ns5:DisplayName>Custom property</ns5:DisplayName><ns5:Name /><ns5:Value i:type="c:string" /></ns3:anyType></ns2:Properties><ns2:TypeId>DRAWINGSURFACE</ns2:TypeId><Borders /><Header>Diagram 1</Header><Lines /><Zoom>0.55</Zoom></DrawingSurfaceModel></DrawingSurfaceList><MetaInformation><Assumptions>1. The application is deployed in a single Docker host with the SQLite file mounted as a named volume; this is acceptable for a coursework demo but not for production scale.
2. TLS certificates are self-signed and accepted manually by the grader's browser; the private key never leaves the demo host.
3. GitHub Actions runners are trusted; secrets used for the pipeline live in GitHub repository secrets, not in code.
4. There is no external SMTP, payment, or identity provider in scope; all authentication is local username/password.</Assumptions><Contributors>Ammar Maqdoom, Ikramah Elahi, Muhammad Mustafa</Contributors><ExternalDependencies>Python 3.12, Flask 3.x, Flask-Login, Flask-WTF, Flask-SQLAlchemy, bcrypt, gunicorn 21.x, Nginx 1.27, SQLite 3.x, Docker Engine 24+, GitHub Actions (ubuntu-latest), Bandit, Semgrep, pip-audit, OWASP ZAP baseline.</ExternalDependencies><HighLevelSystemDescription>Cyber Vuln Shop is a Flask-based e-commerce demo with public catalog, authenticated customer flows (cart, checkout, profile, orders), and an admin area for user/product/order management. It is deployed in Docker behind an Nginx TLS proxy and protected by Flask-Login session auth, Flask-WTF CSRF protection, bcrypt password hashing, and a parameterised SQLAlchemy ORM. A separately-deployed vulnerable replica intentionally exposes ten OWASP Top 10 flaws so the same DFD can be analysed in both the vulnerable and hardened states.</HighLevelSystemDescription><Owner>Ammar Maqdoom, Ikramah Elahi, Muhammad Mustafa</Owner><Reviewer>Habib University - Cyber Security Course</Reviewer><ThreatModelName>Cyber Vuln Shop</ThreatModelName></MetaInformation><Notes /><ThreatInstances><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sd03ea74a1fac4f6aa7ae7bbf731a0a00-bff4cb3878eb</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>9163171f-f881-4b33-8e4c-fef5cdf0f7b9</FlowGuid><Id>T1</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>High</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T1: Weak password policy enables account takeover</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Spoofing</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Registration accepts any password length, allowing trivial brute force or credential stuffing.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>An attacker registers (or guesses) an account with a one-character password and reuses the credential against other sites. Without a minimum-length policy, the auth boundary collapses in seconds. Mitigation: enforce &gt;=8 character passwords (implemented) and add login lockout / rate limiting.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>routes/auth.py register</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>High</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T1: Weak password policy enables account takeover</Title><TypeId>SP</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Spoofing</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>An attacker registers (or guesses) an account with a one-character password and reuses the credential against other sites. Without a minimum-length policy, the auth boundary collapses in seconds. Mitigation: enforce &gt;=8 character passwords (implemented) and add login lockout / rate limiting.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Registration accepts any password length, allowing trivial brute force or credential stuffing.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sacb4a8c0200949e78ec8d9b7464230fb-ce7f368ae6cf</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>5f56dcf1-a8de-440a-9f9a-21920dcb3a55</FlowGuid><Id>T2</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Critical</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T2: Unauthenticated dump of user records including MD5 hashes</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Information Disclosure</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Vulnerable replica exposes /users.json with MD5 password hashes that can be cracked offline.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>An attacker calls /users.json unauthenticated and receives every account's bcrypt hash, MD5 hash, e-mail and role. MD5 is rainbow-table ready, so even weak passwords (T1) crack in seconds. Mitigation: remove the endpoint, never store legacy hashes, require admin role.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py /api/admin/users and users.avatar</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Critical</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T2: Unauthenticated dump of user records including MD5 hashes</Title><TypeId>IN</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Information Disclosure</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>An attacker calls /users.json unauthenticated and receives every account's bcrypt hash, MD5 hash, e-mail and role. MD5 is rainbow-table ready, so even weak passwords (T1) crack in seconds. Mitigation: remove the endpoint, never store legacy hashes, require admin role.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Vulnerable replica exposes /users.json with MD5 password hashes that can be cracked offline.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>S4e065514bc85489ea14218f218eced51-48b2a00339ed</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>3d05544f-b15f-4ddd-b6fd-64e01e14b81f</FlowGuid><Id>T3</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>High</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T3: RBAC bypass on /admin endpoints</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Elevation of Privilege</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Authenticated non-admin users reach admin pages because the guard only checks login state.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>A regular customer browses to /admin and manages users, products, or orders because the vulnerable build only verifies is_authenticated, not role. Mitigation: enforce current_user.is_admin in the admin_required decorator (implemented).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>routes/admin.py admin_required</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>High</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T3: RBAC bypass on /admin endpoints</Title><TypeId>EL</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Elevation of Privilege</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>A regular customer browses to /admin and manages users, products, or orders because the vulnerable build only verifies is_authenticated, not role. Mitigation: enforce current_user.is_admin in the admin_required decorator (implemented).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Authenticated non-admin users reach admin pages because the guard only checks login state.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Saf54ae5ad2f546559999a289479a3bae-ea97d27778d0</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>def25183-04c2-4c76-a608-46ca6913f847</FlowGuid><Id>T4</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>High</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T4: CSRF on profile and cart state changes</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Tampering</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Forms accept POSTs without a CSRF token, enabling cross-site forced actions.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>A logged-in user visits an attacker page that auto-submits a form to /profile/update; the browser sends the session cookie and the account is taken over. Mitigation: Flask-WTF CSRFProtect + csrf_token() on all state-changing forms (implemented).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>routes/profile.py update_profile</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>High</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T4: CSRF on profile and cart state changes</Title><TypeId>TA</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Tampering</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>A logged-in user visits an attacker page that auto-submits a form to /profile/update; the browser sends the session cookie and the account is taken over. Mitigation: Flask-WTF CSRFProtect + csrf_token() on all state-changing forms (implemented).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Forms accept POSTs without a CSRF token, enabling cross-site forced actions.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sdd376a4e912047d098f97b56d4ef9b11-19fbfe33be06</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>285c286d-61dd-47a3-b9dc-f468f33e9e32</FlowGuid><Id>T5</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Medium</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T5: Open redirect on login next parameter</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Spoofing</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Login redirects to attacker-controlled URLs supplied via ?next=.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Attacker emails a phishing link of the form /auth/login?next=https://evil.tld; after credentials, the victim lands on the attacker site inside the trusted brand context. Mitigation: validate next is a relative URL on the same host (implemented).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>routes/auth.py login next= parameter</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Medium</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T5: Open redirect on login next parameter</Title><TypeId>SP</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Spoofing</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>Attacker emails a phishing link of the form /auth/login?next=https://evil.tld; after credentials, the victim lands on the attacker site inside the trusted brand context. Mitigation: validate next is a relative URL on the same host (implemented).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Login redirects to attacker-controlled URLs supplied via ?next=.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>S47b8601b6f4e44d4b5beda4180cf386b-98da5fb6b2d6</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>fceb839a-71eb-4d5a-9b09-33e3aaf0ec3e</FlowGuid><Id>T6</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Critical</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T6: SQL injection in product search</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Tampering</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>User input concatenated into raw SQL; UNION SELECT exfiltrates user table.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Payload %' UNION SELECT id, email, '', 0, 0, '', '' FROM users-- causes the vulnerable search endpoint to display every user e-mail as a product. Mitigation: parameterised ORM queries (SQLAlchemy filter) and input validation (implemented in fixed build).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py /products/ search</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Critical</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T6: SQL injection in product search</Title><TypeId>TA</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Tampering</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>Payload %' UNION SELECT id, email, '', 0, 0, '', '' FROM users-- causes the vulnerable search endpoint to display every user e-mail as a product. Mitigation: parameterised ORM queries (SQLAlchemy filter) and input validation (implemented in fixed build).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>User input concatenated into raw SQL; UNION SELECT exfiltrates user table.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>S4ff7f257963045128b3c6ec09f991560-9cf5c9a8868b</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>0d8c03ed-e280-41e5-89aa-ca50f18bae55</FlowGuid><Id>T7</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>High</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T7: Stored XSS in product reviews</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Tampering</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Review text rendered with |safe; &lt;script&gt; persists and runs in every viewer.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>An attacker submits &lt;script&gt;document.title='XSS_FIRED'&lt;/script&gt; as a review; the page title changes proving execution. Mitigation: rely on Jinja2 default autoescaping, remove |safe (implemented in fixed build).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py /products/&lt;id&gt;</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>High</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T7: Stored XSS in product reviews</Title><TypeId>TA</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Tampering</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>An attacker submits &lt;script&gt;document.title='XSS_FIRED'&lt;/script&gt; as a review; the page title changes proving execution. Mitigation: rely on Jinja2 default autoescaping, remove |safe (implemented in fixed build).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Review text rendered with |safe; &lt;script&gt; persists and runs in every viewer.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sd6e0c53af4af4b9894c0c6ba7e53d733-bb7133d1c8b9</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>aa5f73d4-3c75-498a-9b28-65f10d990dde</FlowGuid><Id>T8</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>High</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T8: IDOR on order details</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Information Disclosure</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Any authenticated user can read any order by changing the URL ID.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Bob (id 3) browses /orders/1 and reads Alice's order with banner 'Customer ID: 2 (current user ID: 3)'. Mitigation: enforce Order.user_id == current_user.id in the route (implemented).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py /orders/&lt;id&gt;</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>High</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T8: IDOR on order details</Title><TypeId>IN</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Information Disclosure</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>Bob (id 3) browses /orders/1 and reads Alice's order with banner 'Customer ID: 2 (current user ID: 3)'. Mitigation: enforce Order.user_id == current_user.id in the route (implemented).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Any authenticated user can read any order by changing the URL ID.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sd619b7bbed6e4348b0c9b5e7f2026ead-4e7d2457059a</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>55edee46-2b1f-4369-8ef9-9c6cedf1e48b</FlowGuid><Id>T9</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Critical</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T9: Debug endpoint leaks secrets</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Information Disclosure</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Unauthenticated /debug returns environment variables including secret keys.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>/debug exposes EXPOSED_ADMIN_API_KEY, JWT_SECRET, LLM_API_KEY, SECRET_KEY. With the JWT secret, an attacker forges admin tokens. Mitigation: remove debug endpoint, store secrets in env vars only.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py /debug</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Critical</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T9: Debug endpoint leaks secrets</Title><TypeId>IN</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Information Disclosure</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>/debug exposes EXPOSED_ADMIN_API_KEY, JWT_SECRET, LLM_API_KEY, SECRET_KEY. With the JWT secret, an attacker forges admin tokens. Mitigation: remove debug endpoint, store secrets in env vars only.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Unauthenticated /debug returns environment variables including secret keys.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>S3f87776a8b3e4d49853bc168d4dad58b-4bfdd2b7ec9c</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>a0455f31-c359-40b4-a45f-6d59b9c42ab8</FlowGuid><Id>T10</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Critical</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T10: Unauthenticated bulk database export</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Information Disclosure</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>/api/export returns the full SQLite contents without authentication.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Any internet client receives users, orders, products, reviews, and cart contents in a single response. Mitigation: remove endpoint; any export must require admin role and be audit-logged.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py /api/export</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Critical</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T10: Unauthenticated bulk database export</Title><TypeId>IN</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Information Disclosure</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>Any internet client receives users, orders, products, reviews, and cart contents in a single response. Mitigation: remove endpoint; any export must require admin role and be audit-logged.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>/api/export returns the full SQLite contents without authentication.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sc00d61858e6c494b8837199184cb775a-c1063687c69c</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>4ced55b1-1b20-456c-bc74-7a736edb9d5a</FlowGuid><Id>T11</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Medium</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T11: Verbose error pages expose stack traces</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Information Disclosure</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Unhandled exceptions render full Werkzeug debugger output.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Stack traces reveal internal paths, frameworks, and SQL fragments that help an attacker chain other findings. Mitigation: production error handler returns a generic page; debug=False in Flask.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py handle_exception</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Medium</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T11: Verbose error pages expose stack traces</Title><TypeId>IN</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Information Disclosure</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>Stack traces reveal internal paths, frameworks, and SQL fragments that help an attacker chain other findings. Mitigation: production error handler returns a generic page; debug=False in Flask.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Unhandled exceptions render full Werkzeug debugger output.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>S3208ced8041a4b19b6034e05722bbc62-a30fa68bb39d</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>4692a8a4-a15b-40cc-a056-d493b1a1b886</FlowGuid><Id>T12</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Medium</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T12: No login lockout / rate limit</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Denial of Service</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Login form accepts unlimited password guesses.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Combined with T1 (weak passwords) this becomes a viable credential stuffing target. Mitigation: temporary lockout after N failed attempts (implemented for the fixed build).</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>demo/vulnerable_app.py login</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Medium</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T12: No login lockout / rate limit</Title><TypeId>DE</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Denial of Service</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>Combined with T1 (weak passwords) this becomes a viable credential stuffing target. Mitigation: temporary lockout after N failed attempts (implemented for the fixed build).</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>Login form accepts unlimited password guesses.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sa5b4c01cf5c446a2b617528821c6bb8f-a46b24828556</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>248e21e1-c435-4824-9837-918ece33857b</FlowGuid><Id>T13</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Medium</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T13: Pipeline ships without quality gates</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Tampering</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>If SAST/SCA/DAST jobs do not fail on high findings, vulnerable code reaches main unnoticed.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>A team member could disable the failure conditions on the security scans and merge to main. Mitigation: scripts/check_zap_high.py + Bandit severity gate fail the job on high findings.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>.github/workflows/devsecops.yml</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Medium</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T13: Pipeline ships without quality gates</Title><TypeId>TA</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Tampering</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>A team member could disable the failure conditions on the security scans and merge to main. Mitigation: scripts/check_zap_high.py + Bandit severity gate fail the job on high findings.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>If SAST/SCA/DAST jobs do not fail on high findings, vulnerable code reaches main unnoticed.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>Sf461b224ae554a9493155f8b49968bda-ea1f5a64b4c8</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>ec954082-8306-4e20-83e7-27256a5380d0</FlowGuid><Id>T14</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Medium</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T14: TLS private key committed to git</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Information Disclosure</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>A developer commits certs/privkey.pem and the demo HTTPS identity is leaked.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>If the self-signed key is added to version control, anybody who clones the repository can impersonate the demo server. Mitigation: certs/ is .gitignored, only certs/.gitkeep is tracked.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>certs/privkey.pem</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Medium</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>ManuallyAdded</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring></Properties><SourceGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</SourceGuid><State>ManuallyAdded</State><StateInformation /><TargetGuid>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</TargetGuid><Title>T14: TLS private key committed to git</Title><TypeId>IN</TypeId><Upgraded>false</Upgraded><UserThreatCategory>Information Disclosure</UserThreatCategory><UserThreatDescription>If the self-signed key is added to version control, anybody who clones the repository can impersonate the demo server. Mitigation: certs/ is .gitignored, only certs/.gitkeep is tracked.</UserThreatDescription><UserThreatShortDescription>A developer commits certs/privkey.pem and the demo HTTPS identity is leaked.</UserThreatShortDescription><Wide>false</Wide></ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><ns3:Key>S29441315fb164e809f5a00940042ffe8-058f76c04688</ns3:Key><ns3:Value><ChangedBy /><DrawingSurfaceGuid>86ef6a47-58c8-471d-99aa-532ffc1cccb6</DrawingSurfaceGuid><FlowGuid>bacfe906-8e6f-47e2-99d2-3f8b4f47a6ef</FlowGuid><Id>T15</Id><InteractionString /><ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</ModifiedAt><Priority>Low</Priority><Properties><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Title</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>T15: Admin actions have no audit trail</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatCategory</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Repudiation</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Destructive product or user changes are not logged.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>UserThreatDescription</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>A malicious or coerced admin can change prices or roles and the team cannot prove who did it after the fact. Mitigation (residual risk): add an audit_log table on a follow-up sprint; currently accepted.</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>InteractionString</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>routes/admin.py</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>Priority</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Low</ns3:Value></ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:KeyValueOfstringstring><ns3:Key>State</ns3:Key><ns3:Value>Manually